    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16817": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty, Sociology",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Part-Time Faculty, Sociology Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm Part-Time Faculty, Sociology Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please attach the following 3 (PDF) documents to your online Faculty Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) CV with contact information for three (3) professional references, as one complete document. (#3) If available, a course syllabus from a sociology course you have taught at the college/university level. Posting Details Position Information Job Title Part-Time Faculty, Sociology Job Description Summary, Duties and Responsibilities, Required Qualifications and Preferred Qualifications As the largest college at the University of Rhode Island, the College of Arts and Sciences brings together and supports undergraduate programs across the arts, humanities, sciences, and social sciences. The college is committed to excellence in teaching and its academic units create learning experiences that stress the free and rigorous pursuit of knowledge, intellectual curiosity, respect for differences across cultures and perspectives, a passion for personal expression, and an investment in the responsibilities of an educated global citizen in a democratic society. The College facilitates students’ development into leaders and global citizens who think critically and creatively, communicate effectively, and participate actively in local and global communities. The Department of Sociology and Anthropology in the College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time faculty to teach sociology courses on a per-course basis. For Fall 2026 we seek someone able to teach SOC100, Introduction to the Sociological Perspective, and/or SOC212, Sociology of the Family. These courses meet in-person on MWF for Fall 2026 and are at the undergraduate level. Applicants may be contacted regarding future instructional opportunities and invited to apply should additional positions become available. KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : A selected candidate will teach one (1) or two (2) courses in the Department of Sociology and Anthropology. Part-time faculty are not required to engage in research or service activities but must hold regular office hours and be available to assist students enrolled in their courses. They are expected to stay current in their fields of instruction and with pedagogical methods and instructional technologies. Most courses are three (3) credits. In-person courses meet for approximately three (3) hours per week over the course of an approximately fourteen (14) week semester. Classes meet during the day at the Kingston campus. QUALIFICATIONS Required: 1. Master’s or doctoral degree in sociology, or a closely related field with substantial background in sociology at time of application. 2. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with individuals from a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. Preferred: 1. Experience teaching sociology or closely related field at the college level. 2. Experience teaching in one or more of the following areas: Introductory sociology, sociology of the family at the college level. ______________________________________________________________________________________________________ All temporary workers are required to successfully complete a background check in accordance with the standards applicable to regular employees in comparable positions. Background check requirements will be determined based on the scope of work, level of access, and associated risk of the role. _______________________________________________________________________________________________________ EEO Statement: URI is unwavering in its commitment to equal opportunity, community, equity, and diversity. In furtherance of its mission to serve and support all learners as the state’s public flagship university, URI continues to expand efforts to recruit and retain a broadly representative workforce as well as to regularly evaluate the effectiveness of those efforts. All employment decisions, including hiring, are made without regard to protected status. Qualified individuals with disabilities may request reasonable accommodation by contacting the URI benefits office at 401-874-2416. For TTY assistance, contact R.I. Relay Services at 711. Union Non-Union Faculty Status Academic Year, Part-time, Limited End Date of Restriction or Limitation NOTE : Part-time faculty are temporary employees under the University’s internal payroll system and are non-state, non-union, non-benefited positions, where the incumbent is at-will and may be subject to termination at any time. Position limited to December 22, 2026 _______________________________________________________________________________________________________ Department Information Department Sociology & Anthropology Contact(s) Please note: Job applications must be submitted directly online only at: (https://jobs.uri.edu) Contact Email Campus Location Kingston Grant Funded No Extension Contingent on Funding Date Special Instructions to Applicants Please attach the following 3 ( PDF ) documents to your online Faculty Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) CV with contact information for three (3) professional references, as one complete document. (#3) If available, a course syllabus from a sociology course you have taught at the college/university level. Posting Information Position Number N/A - Internal Payroll Posting Number F00524 Posting Date 07/02/2026 Closing Date 08/21/2027 Open Until Filled No Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16811 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Sample Syllabi Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Other Chronicle Academic Careers Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed New England Herc The Edu Ledger LinkedIn Asiana In Higher Ed Disable in High ed Blacks In Higher Ed Diversity Jobs HBCU Connect Hispanic in Higher ed LGBT in Higher Ed Native American in Higher ed Woman and Higher Ed Veterans in Higher Edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/17198": {
      "title": "SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH GRANT ASSISTANT",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH GRANT ASSISTANT Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH GRANT ASSISTANT Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This is a full time calendar year position whihc includes full medical benefits including the tuition waiver at URI, CCRI and RIC. For a full list of the benefits package, visit the State of Rhode Island Classified benefits here: https://www.exploreemployeebenefits.ri.gov/ A criminal background check is required for the successful applicant. This requirement will be paid for by the University. If you are a lateral transfer (same title as the posting) within ESP-URI/NEARI please contact Karen Healey in Human Resources at 874-5267 or karen_healey@uri.edu prior to applying to this position . The name of a contact person will be provided for all lateral transfers ONLY to schedule an informational meeting prior to the application deadline date. THIS POSITION IS COVERED BY ESP-URI/NEARI. MEMBERS OF THIS UNION WILL RECEIVE PREFERENTIAL CONSIDERATION. IF THE POSITION IS NOT FILLED BY A CURRENT UNION MEMBER, WE WILL THEN CONSIDER OTHER APPLICANTS FOR THE POSITION. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number CS02322 Job Title SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH GRANT ASSISTANT Position Number 10223 FTE FLSA Pay Rate Salary Range $49,837 - $54,317 Pay Grade Level Grade 316 Position Type Classified Civil Service Staff Union ESP-URI/NEARI (Educational Support Professionals) Status Calendar Year, Full-time, Limited Restriction or Limitation Reason Funding End Date of Restriction or Limitation Limited to 11/27/2027 with anticipation of renewal. Department Information Department GSO Rhode Island Sea Grant Contact(s) Karen G. Healey, MS Specialist, Talent Acquisition Office of Human Resource Administration 80 Lower College Road Kingston, RI 02881 Contact Phone/Extension 401-874-5267 Contact Email karen_healey@uri.edu Physical Demands Campus Location Narragansett Bay Campus Building(s) Grant Funded Extension Contingent on Funding Date Extension Contingency Notes Duties and Responsibilities GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES : At the University of Rhode Island, to assist a Principal Investigator with and provide support related services for the execution of grant funded research endeavors by monitoring and allocating awarded funds, predicting future financial requirements and by providing a high level of clerical support that includes the creating of the final proof of complex submitted research proposals, research reports and scientific papers via the usage of automated office equipment; and to do related work as required. SUPERVISION RECEIVED : Works under the general supervision of a Principal Investigator adhering to established instructions and procedures with wide latitude for the exercise of independent judgment; work is generally in final form when completed but may be checked for accuracy and compliance with prescribed instructions and procedures. SUPERVISION EXERCISED : Supervises and coordinates the work of subordinates providing related research support services. ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLES OF WORK PERFORMED : At the University of Rhode island, to assist a Principal Investigator with and provide support related services for the execution of grant funded research endeavors by monitoring and allocating awarded funds, predicting future financial requirements and by providing a high level of clerical support that includes the creating of the final proof of complex submitted research proposals, research reports and scientific papers via the usage of automated office equipment. To introduce, modify existing or develop new office automation aids required for the successful execution, monitoring and completion of research projects. To prepare graphics, organize data and determine the formats for such data. To maintain records and files. To assist with the development, execution and control of a budget according to established procedures and in the prediction of future financial requirements. To supervise and coordinate the work of subordinates providing related research support services. To do related work as required. Required Qualifications REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS FOR APPOINTMENT : KNOWLEDGE , SKILLS AND CAPACITIES : A thorough knowledge of the operation of office systems and software packages utilized for creating, editing and storing of scientific research documents, corresponding graphics and awarded funds; a familiarity with the principles and practices utilized for budget preparation including the ability to predict future financial requirements; a thorough knowledge of general office practices, commercial arithmetic and business English; the ability to research and develop new or modify existing office automation aids required for the successful completion of research projects; the ability to maintain files and records; the ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with superiors, associates and other employees; the ability to supervise, coordinate and review the work of subordinates assigned to assist; and related capacities and abilities. EDUCATION AND EXPERIENCE : EDUCATION : Such as may have been gained through: graduation from a two year college including courses in basic accounting, office practice, typing and the operation of automated office equipment and supplemented by courses in engineering or physical/biological sciences; and EXPERIENCE : Such as may have been gained through: employment in a responsible clerical position involving the performance of office tasks inclusive of complex automatic typing and basic accounting. OR: any combination of education and experience that shall be substantially equivalent to the above education and experience. Preferred Qualifications Environmental Conditions This position is not substantially exposed to adverse environmental conditions Work Hours 8:30 AM to 4:30 PM — Monday through Friday Days Off: Saturday & Sunday Posting Date 10/10/2026 Closing Date 10/20/2026 Special Instructions to Applicants This is a full time calendar year position whihc includes full medical benefits including the tuition waiver at URI , CCRI and RIC . For a full list of the benefits package, visit the State of Rhode Island Classified benefits here: https://www.exploreemployeebenefits.ri.gov/ A criminal background check is required for the successful applicant. This requirement will be paid for by the University. If you are a lateral transfer (same title as the posting) within ESP - URI / NEARI please contact Karen Healey in Human Resources at 874-5267 or karen_healey@uri.edu prior to applying to this position . The name of a contact person will be provided for all lateral transfers ONLY to schedule an informational meeting prior to the application deadline date. THIS POSITION IS COVERED BY ESP - URI / NEARI . MEMBERS OF THIS UNION WILL RECEIVE PREFERENTIAL CONSIDERATION . IF THE POSITION IS NOT FILLED BY A CURRENT UNION MEMBER , WE WILL THEN CONSIDER OTHER APPLICANTS FOR THE POSITION . Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/17182 Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Have you graduated from either a two or four year college? Yes No * Have you taken course(s) in basic accounting? Yes No * Have you taken any courses in engineering, physical/biological science? Yes No * Have you been employed in a responsible clerical position involving the performance of office tasks inclusive of COMPLEX automatic typing and BASIC ACCOUNTING? Yes No * Are you currently a State employee within Union ESP-URI/NEARI? Yes - I understand that if I am a lateral transfer and the senior most bidder, I must accept this position. Please contact Karen Healey in HR with questions about this at (401)874-5267. No To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/17029": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate General Counsel - (3 Positions)",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate General Counsel - (3 Positions) Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm Assistant/Associate General Counsel - (3 Positions) Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please attach the following 2 (PDF) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number SF02195 Job Title Assistant/Associate General Counsel - (3 Positions) Position Number 109688 & 108454 & PS#00008199 FTE 3.0 FLSA Exempt Position Type Professional Staff Union Non-Union Non-Classified Staff Pay Grade Level Grade Levels: 15 & 16 Pay Grade Range Salary Hiring Range for the Assistant grade: 15: ($135,000-$155,000) and Salary Hiring Range for the Associate grade: 16: ($160,000-$180,000) Status Calendar Year, Full-time, Permanent Department Information Department Office of General Counsel Contact(s) Please note: Job applications must be submitted directly online only at: (https://jobs.uri.edu) Contact Phone/Extension Contact Email Physical Demands Campus Location Kingston Grant Funded No Extension Contingency Notes Job Description Summary The search will remain open until the position has been filled. First consideration will be given to applications received by September 26, 2026. Applications received after September 26, 2026 may be reviewed depending on search progress and needs but are not guaranteed full consideration. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ About URI : The University of Rhode Island enrolls approximately 17,000 students across its graduate and undergraduate programs and is the State’s flagship public research university, as well as the land grant and sea grant university, for the state of Rhode Island. The main campus is located in the historic village of Kingston, and the Bay Campus is located in Narragansett. Both campuses are near major beaches in a beautiful coastal community. URI is just 30 minutes from Providence, RI and within easy reach of Newport, Boston, and New York City. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ THE POSITION WILL BE EITHER AN ASSISTANT GENERAL COUNSEL ( GRADE :15) OR AN ASSOCIATE GENERAL COUNSEL ( GRADE : 16), AND CAN BE EXPECTED TO BE FILLED AT THE RESPECTIVE LEVELS DEPENDING ON QUALIFICATIONS AND EXPERIENCE . ________________________________________________________________________________________________ THE POSITIONS ARE ELIGIBLE FOR HYBRID OR IN- PERSON WORK SCHEDULE . POSITION SUMMARY Assists the General Counsel of the University in providing legal services to the University community and the University President and Board of Trustees as may be required. Performs standard legal work and/or participates in the performance of wide-ranging, fast-paced, and complex legal work involving multiple stakeholders to include such areas as governance and open meetings, research and sponsored programs, policy review, legal compliance issues, contract reviews, real property, employment, labor, intellectual property, privacy and confidentiality, complex transactions, student affairs, athletics, public records, purchasing, civil rights, and academic affairs. Participates and/or assists in the conduct of litigation as assigned. Duties and Responsibilities KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : This job description has been designed to indicate the general nature and level of work performed by employees within this classification at the direction of the General Counsel. It is not designed to contain or be interpreted as a comprehensive inventory of all duties and responsibilities required of employees assigned to the job. Essential Functions: Review and maintain currency with laws, rules, and regulations affecting higher education and changes to University policy and keep current with legal implications of those changes. Anticipate, identify, and help to resolve legal and strategic risk management issues. Provide well-rounded expert advice to management in order to develop legal strategies and solutions. Draft and review policies and procedures, regulations, bylaws, and other documents within legal specialty areas. Research and analyze laws and regulations and prepare legal documents that include memoranda and correspondence. Educate and train the University community about legal issues. The Asst. General Counsel will be expected to conduct legal research and analysis. Prepare written and verbal opinions of counsel as needed. Associate General Counsel will be expected to do all prior roles listed in addition to supervising these responsibilities of others. Review, prepare, and approve for legal sufficiency contracts, including form and template agreements. Coordinate legal services to departments and divisions within areas of legal specialty. Represent the University before agencies, boards, committees, commissions, and courts in administrative hearings and litigation and/or oversee such representation. Negotiate settlements involving legal issues or transactions on behalf of the University in conjunction with other University officials and Assistant General Counsel will consult other attorneys within the Office of the General Counsel. Associate General Counsel will supervise support staff, law student interns or externs, and/or outside counsel on a project basis. Represent the Office of the General Counsel at meetings and on committees, task forces, and work groups as assigned. Assist the General Counsel by providing client-focused and mission-oriented legal services to the departments, colleges, officials, and employees of the University and perform such other and related duties as directed by the General Counsel. OTHER DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Perform other related duties as required. LICENSES , TOOLS , AND EQUIPMENT : Individually assigned computers, printers; word processing, spreadsheet, and database software; Westlaw and other online legal research platforms. License to practice law in the state of Rhode Island and be a member of the Rhode Island Bar Association at the time of appointment, or eligible and willing to obtain such admission if also a member in good standing of the bar of any other state and eligible to register with the Rhode Island Supreme Court as an “in-house counsel”. Required Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED : 1. Graduation from an accredited law school. 2. License to practice law in the state of Rhode Island and be a member of the Rhode Island Bar Association at the time of appointment, or eligible and willing to obtain such admission if also a member in good standing of the bar of any other state and eligible to register with the Rhode Island Supreme Court as an “in-house counsel.” 3. Candidates being considered for the Asst. General Counsel should have a minimum of three years relevant experience as a practicing attorney, or two years of direct relevant experience representing a large university, public sector entity, or similarly complex organization or institution. Candidates being considered for Assoc. General Counsel should have a minimum of five years relevant experience as a practicing attorney, or three years of direct relevant experience representing a large university, public sector entity, or similarly complex organization or institution. 4. Experience in legal research and writing. 5. Written, oral, negotiation, and presentation skills, including the ability to provide clear, practical, risk-informed legal advice. 6. Ability to work effectively with a wide range of institutional stakeholders, including governing bodies, senior leaders, administrators, faculty, staff, and external parties. 7. Collaborative and flexible work style, and willingness to take on a broad range of responsibilities. 8. Ability to independently manage a substantial and diverse legal portfolio and prioritize competing demands in a fast paced environment. Preferred Qualifications PREFERRED : 1. Experience providing legal counsel to institution(s) of higher education. 2. Experience in one or more legal areas commonly encountered at public research universities, such as employment law, labor relations, civil rights, constitutional protections, academic affairs, student affairs, athletics, research compliance, data privacy, procurement, or real estate. 3. Proficiency in utilizing traditional and emerging technologies, such as legal research, matter management, workflow automation, and practice tools. 4. Candidates being considered for Assoc. General counsel: Experience developing or implementing process improvements that increase efficiency, consistency, and client service within a legal environment. ______________________________________________________________________________________________________ EEO Statement: URI is unwavering in its commitment to equal opportunity, community, equity, and diversity. In furtherance of its mission to serve and support all learners as the state’s public flagship university, URI continues to expand efforts to recruit and retain a broadly representative workforce as well as to regularly evaluate the effectiveness of those efforts. All employment decisions, including hiring, are made without regard to protected status. Qualified individuals with disabilities may request reasonable accommodation by contacting the URI benefits office at 401-874-2416. For TTY assistance, contact R.I. Relay Services at 711. Environmental Conditions ENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS : This position is not substantially exposed to adverse environmental conditions. Posting Date 08/26/2026 Closing Date Special Instructions to Applicants Please attach the following 2 ( PDF ) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16913 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Resume Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you a graduate of an accredited law school? Yes No * Do you have a License to practice law in the state of Rhode Island and be a member of the Rhode Island Bar Association at the time of appointment, or eligible and willing to obtain such admission if also a member in good standing of the bar of any other state and eligible to register with the Rhode Island Supreme Court as an “in-house counsel”? Yes No * Do you have a minimum of three years relevant experience as a practicing attorney, or two years of direct relevant experience representing a large university, public sector entity, or similarly complex organization or institution and/or do you have a minimum of five years relevant experience as a practicing attorney, or three years of direct relevant experience representing a large university, public sector entity, or similarly complex organization or institution? Yes No * Do you have experience in legal research and writing? Yes No * Do you have written, oral, negotiation, and presentation skills, including the ability to provide clear, practical, risk-informed legal advice? Yes No * Do you have ability to work effectively with a wide range of institutional stakeholders, including governing bodies, senior leaders, administrators, faculty, staff, and external parties? Yes No * Do you have collaborative and flexible work style, and willingness to take on a broad range of responsibilities? Yes No * Do you have ability to independently manage a substantial and diverse legal portfolio and prioritize competing demands in a fast-paced environment? Yes No How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Other Chronicle Academic Careers Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed New England Herc The Edu Ledger LinkedIn Asiana In Higher Ed Disable in High ed Blacks In Higher Ed Diversity Jobs HBCU Connect Hispanic in Higher ed LGBT in Higher Ed Native American in Higher ed Woman and Higher Ed Veterans in Higher Edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16861": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Disability Access &amp; Student Support",
      "description": "University Of Rhode Island Applicant Portal | Coordinator, Disability Access &amp; Student Support Skip to Main Content University Of Rhode Island H uman R esource A dministration 80 Lower College Road, Kingston, RI 02881 urihr@ucs.uri.edu - 401.874.2416 - 401.874.5741 (fax) URI Homepage Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help About URI Office of Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Campus Maps & Directions Hours: Monday - Friday 8.30am to 4.30pm Coordinator, Disability Access & Student Support Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please attach the following 2 (PDF) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number SF02179 Job Title Coordinator, Disability Access & Student Support Position Number 109328 FTE 1.00 FLSA Exempt Position Type Professional Staff Union PSA/NEA - Professional Staff Assoc Pay Grade Level Grade Level: 11 Pay Grade Range Anticipated Hiring Salary Range: $70,000 - $75,000 Status Calendar Year, Full-time, Permanent Department Information Department VP Student Affrs. Contact(s) Please note: Job applications must be submitted directly online only at: (https://jobs.uri.edu) Contact Phone/Extension Contact Email Physical Demands Campus Location Kingston Grant Funded No Extension Contingency Notes Job Description Summary The search will remain open until the position has been filled. First consideration will be given to applications received by September 14, 2026. Applications received after September 14, 2026 may be reviewed depending on search progress and needs but are not guaranteed full consideration. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ About URI : The University of Rhode Island enrolls approximately 17,000 students across its graduate and undergraduate programs and is the State’s flagship public research university, as well as the land grant and sea grant university, for the state of Rhode Island. The main campus is located in the historic village of Kingston, and the Bay Campus is located in Narragansett. Both campuses are near major beaches in a beautiful coastal community. URI is just 30 minutes from Providence, RI and within easy reach of Newport, Boston, and New York City. ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ POSITION SUMMARY : Support students through challenges, connect them to appropriate campus and community resources, and empower/ promote agency to enhance well-being . Collaboratively coordinate and deliver university accessibility services and support for students with disabilities. Duties and Responsibilities KEY DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Under the supervision of the Assistant Deans, implement case management and individual intervention strategies for students needing behavioral intervention (i.e. , disruptive students, substance abuse prevention, emotional well-being, and safety. ) Services will be provided to students during in—person and/or remote meetings . Establish and supervise delivery and database documentation of essential services to students with disabilities, including reasonable accommodations to support equal opportunity in URI academic and co—curricular programs and services. Serve as a liaison with Housing and Residential Life to support students with documented disability needs and/or housing insecurity. Serve in a triage/ referral capacity to provide support and assessment for the type of resources and assistance needed for academic and personal success, including appropriate disability—related options. Examples include mental health concerns, medical emergencies, family challenges, food insecurity, financial hardship, and bereavement . Assist and advise the Assistant Deans and the DAI / SSAS Teams in guiding University departments regarding students of concern along with accessibility options in courses, programs, and services; supply guidance for providing resources and services to students with disabilities and/or students of concern. Design and implement individual, group, and environmental interventions to increase awareness of mental health problems, available supports and decreases impact of disruptive student behavior on all aspects of campus life. Provide follow—up case management support for students referred to through the University Student Assistance Teams, and for students’ post—hospitalization. Work directly with students and coordinate with appropriate university personnel including University Police, Counseling Center, Health Services, Retention and Academic Success, Violence Prevention and Advocacy Services, Talent Development, Academic Deans, as well as local hospitals and police agencies OTHER DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES : Serve as a trainer in areas of disruptive student behavior and student mental health concerns for faculty and staff. Compile statistical reports and documents for the Dean of Students Office . Represent the Office on University, Division, and other committees, and at University events, such as commencement, new student orientation, open houses and welcome days. Perform other duties as required. LICENSES , TOOLS AND EQUIPMENT : Personal computers, printers, word processing, database management and spreadsheet software. Required Qualifications QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED : Master’s degree in social work, counseling, psychology or college student personnel. Minimum of two years of administrative or instructional experience working with college students in a higher education setting. Ability to manage highly sensitive and confidential communications. Crisis management experience. Experience with issues of disability. Working knowledge of Federal Regulations 504, Americans with Disabilities Act Amendment Act, and EERPA . Experience with accommodation determinations. Strong verbal and interpersonal communication skills. Proficiency in written communication skills. Understanding of student development theory. Knowledge of common issues and challenges facing a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students. Ability to work in a fast—paced, collaborative team environment. Willingness to work nonstandard hours (e.g. , evenings, weekends, late nights). Experience with technology (i.e. Word, Excel, PowerPoint) : Decision—making skills. Ability to work with diverse groups/ populations . Preferred Qualifications PREFERRED : Experience with Student Management software ( i . e. Maxient, Starfish, or similar software). ________________________________________________________________________________________________________ EEO Statement: URI is unwavering in its commitment to equal opportunity, community, equity, and diversity. In furtherance of its mission to serve and support all learners as the state’s public flagship university, URI continues to expand efforts to recruit and retain a broadly representative workforce as well as to regularly evaluate the effectiveness of those efforts. All employment decisions, including hiring, are made without regard to protected status. Qualified individuals with disabilities may request reasonable accommodation by contacting the URI benefits office at 401-874-2416. For TTY assistance, contact R.I. Relay Services at 711. Environmental Conditions ENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS : This position is not substantially exposed to adverse environmental conditions . Posting Date 07/17/2026 Closing Date Special Instructions to Applicants Please attach the following 2 ( PDF ) documents to your online Employment Application: (#1) Cover letter. (#2) Resume. Note: References will be upon request by the search committee. Quicklink for Posting https://jobs.uri.edu/postings/16847 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Resume Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a Master's degree in social work, counseling, psychology or college student personnel? Yes No * Do you have a minimum of two years of administrative or instructional experience working with college students in a higher education setting? Yes No * Do you have ability to manage highly sensitive and confidential communications? Yes No * Do you have crisis management experience? Yes No * Do you have experience with issues of disability? Yes No * Do you have working knowledge of Federal Regulations 504, Americans with Disabilities Act Amendment Act, and EERPA? Yes No * Do you have experience with accommodation determinations? Yes No * Do you have strong verbal and interpersonal communication skills? Yes No * Do you have proficiency in written communication skills? Yes No * Do you have understanding of student development theory? Yes No * Do you have knowledge of common issues and challenges facing a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students? Yes No * Do you have ability to work in a fast—paced, collaborative team environment? Yes No * Do you have willingness to work non-standard hours (e.g. , evenings, weekends, late nights)? Yes No * Do you have experience with technology (i.e. Word, Excel, PowerPoint) : Decision—making skills? Yes No * Do you have ability to work with diverse groups/ populations? Yes No * Are you a URI employee currently in a position covered by the PSA Union? (You will not be disqualified for answering \"No\"). Yes No How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Other Chronicle Academic Careers Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed New England Herc The Edu Ledger LinkedIn Asiana In Higher Ed Disable in High ed Blacks In Higher Ed Diversity Jobs HBCU Connect Hispanic in Higher ed LGBT in Higher Ed Native American in Higher ed Woman and Higher Ed Veterans in Higher Edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
